I"<p>This is probably the simplest and quite an effective antenna you can make for your handheld radio. It is simply a quarter-wave length piece of wire for the 2-meter band, which operates on the 70 cm band on the third harmonic. You will need an SMA connector for an RG58 cable (with a wider flange https://allegro.pl/listing?string=sma%20rg58 ), 50 cm of NITINOL wire – a nickel-titanium shape-memory alloy that allows the wire to return to its original form even after being tied in a knot :-) – and some heat shrink tubing (I used a glow-in-the-dark one because it looks awesome).</p>

<ol>
  <li>Solder the wire to the center pin of the connector.</li>
  <li>Put the heat shrink tubing over the soldered joint and shrink it.</li>
  <li>Insert the finished element into the housing; for greater durability, add a little bit of two-part epoxy glue.</li>
  <li>Put on the 3D-printed cap.</li>
  <li>Enjoy your great antenna!</li>
</ol>

<p>You can find the 3D printing files here: https://www.thingiverse.com/thing:6810945</p>
